.TH HSH 1 "August 2026" "hsh 1.8" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command line interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a small UNIX command line interpreter inspired by
.BR sh (1).
It reads commands from standard input, parses their arguments and
redirections, searches the PATH for executable programs, creates child
processes, and executes external commands.
Beyond a single command,
.B hsh
understands command lists, pipelines, redirections, and here-documents.
.SH PROMPT
In interactive mode,
.B hsh
displays
.B ($)
before reading each command.
The prompt is not displayed when standard input is not a terminal.
.SH COMMAND SEARCH
For a command without a slash,
.B hsh
searches each directory listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable.
If a command contains a slash, it is executed directly.
.SH COMMAND LISTS
.TP
.B ;
Runs the next command unconditionally.
.TP
.B &&
Runs the next command only if the previous command succeeded (exit status 0).
.TP
.B ||
Runs the next command only if the previous command failed (a non-zero exit
status).
.SH PIPELINES
A
.B |
between two commands connects the standard output of the command on the
left to the standard input of the command on the right.
Any number of commands may be chained this way; each command in the
pipeline runs in its own process.
The exit status of a pipeline is the exit status of its last command.
.SH REDIRECTION
.TP
.BI > " file"
Redirects standard output to
.IR file ,
creating it if needed and truncating it if it already exists.
.TP
.BI >> " file"
Redirects standard output to
.IR file ,
appending to it if it already exists.
.TP
.BI < " file"
Redirects standard input to read from
.IR file .
.TP
.BI << " delimiter"
Reads a here-document: lines are read from standard input until a line
matching
.I delimiter
exactly is found, and the collected lines become the standard input of the
command.
.SH BUILTINS
.SS exit
.B exit
terminates the shell.
A numeric argument is used as the exit status.
.SS cd
.B cd [path]
changes the current working directory.
Without an argument,
.B cd
uses
.BR HOME .
The command
.B cd -
changes to the previous working directory.
After a successful directory change,
.B PWD
and
.B OLDPWD
are updated for commands executed by the shell.
.SS env
.B env
prints the current environment.
.SS setenv
.BI "setenv " VARIABLE " " VALUE
creates
.I VARIABLE
in the environment, or overwrites its value if it already exists.
.SS unsetenv
.BI "unsetenv " VARIABLE
removes
.I VARIABLE
from the environment.
.SH EOF
The shell exits when it receives end-of-file on standard input.
In an interactive terminal this is normally produced by
.B Ctrl-D
on an empty input line.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT is handled so that an interactive Ctrl-C does not terminate the shell.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The shell does not implement quoting, command substitution, wildcard
expansion, or variable expansion.
.SH EXIT STATUS
An external command normally returns its exit status.
A command that cannot be found returns 127.
A command that cannot be executed because of permissions returns 126.
A command killed by a signal returns 128 plus the signal number.
A redirection that cannot be opened returns 2, and reports the error in the
same format as
.BR sh (1).
The exit status of a pipeline is the exit status of its last command.
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the Holberton School system and blockchain specialization.